The long-chunk documents loss sits in how each page is compressed, not in the decoder (the model that reads the compressed slots and answers). Both seeds pass the test; it stays provisional until the read-only checker re-derives it.

**What was tested.** Two finetuned models differ only in the chunk length used in their first, text-only training stage: 1,024 tokens versus 256. Each was scored on 1,500 document questions. The score is Qwen3-8B judge accuracy, paired on the same questions; pt = 0.01 accuracy.

| what the decoder sees | long minus short chunks, seed 42 (95% CI) | seed 43 (95% CI) |
|---|---|---|
| all 4 pages compressed (earlier campaign) | −6.2 pt [−8.6, −3.9] | −4.3 pt [−6.7, −1.8] |
| only the page with the answer, compressed | −6.7 pt [−8.9, −4.5] | −4.9 pt [−7.1, −2.5] |
| page the model picks goes in uncompressed, other 3 compressed | +0.1 pt [−0.8, +1.1] | −0.1 pt [−1.1, +0.9] |

- Both models pick the right page on 98.5% of questions. So the long-chunk model's compressed pages still show where the answer is, but carry the answer itself worse.
- With the picked page uncompressed, both models score about .825, above the .809 of the plain 4B model reading all 4 pages uncompressed.
- All four runs were healthy: 1,500 rows each, every row judged, no unreadable verdicts, code unchanged.
- The prediction written before the campaign ("the deficit sits in the compressed page") is now marked confirmed in the campaign rules.
- Each finding is in `docs/longdoc_diagnosis.md`, next to the training change it points to: keep the first-stage chunk length at what later stages compress, or mix short chunks back in. That training choice is yours, later.

**Launched this wake (4 jobs, the per-wake limit):**
- **Text-recovery tool check (191169, running):** a check that my adapted version of the older probe reproduces its recorded 70.5% word recovery, within ±2 pt. That probe asks how many of a page's words the model can rebuild from its compressed slots.
- **Reference page text (191170, running):** 501 pages read by the uncompressed model, the yardstick for that probe.
- **Plumbing run on the newer model format (191171, done, passed):** it rebuilt 20 of 20 pages as readable text.
- **New first-stage rebuild probe (191172, done, passed):** I wrote it this wake. It asks how well each model rebuilds held-out web documents from their slots. With the wrong document's slots the loss jumps from 0.011 to 2.6 nats per token, so the check works.
